    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Spec point** — `spcpt_N9WmDYvbMkKDKgr2`",
    "reading_order" : 2,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000003",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Amide Formation",
    "reading_order" : 3,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 2,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000004",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Amides **are organic compounds with an -CONR<sub>2</sub> functional group",
    "reading_order" : 4,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000005",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "They can be prepared from the **condensation reaction **between an **acyl chloride **and **ammonia **or **amine**",
    "reading_order" : 5,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000006",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "In a **condensation **reaction, two organic molecules **join together **and in the process **eliminate **a small molecule",
    "reading_order" : 6,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000007",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "In this case, the acyl chlorides and ammonia or amine **join together **to form an **amide **and **eliminate **an HCl molecule",
    "reading_order" : 7,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000008",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Condensation reaction",
    "reading_order" : 8,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 4,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000009",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The chlorine atom in acyl chlorides is **electronegative **and draws electron density from the carbonyl carbon",
    "reading_order" : 9,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000010",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The carbonyl carbon is therefore **electron-deficient **and can be attacked by **nucleophiles**",
    "reading_order" : 10,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000011",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The nitrogen atom in ammonia and amines has a lone pair of electrons which can act as a **nucleophile **and attack the carbonyl carbon",
    "reading_order" : 11,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000012",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "As a result, the C-Cl bond is **broken **and an **amide **is formed",
    "reading_order" : 12,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000013",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Whether the product is a **substituted **amide or not, depends on the nature of the **nucleophile**",
    "reading_order" : 13,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000014",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Primary and secondary amines will give a **substituted amide**",
    "reading_order" : 14,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000015",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The reaction of acyl chlorides with ammonia will produce a **non-substituted amide**",
    "reading_order" : 15,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000017",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "***Acyl chlorides undergo condensation reactions with ammonia and amines to form amides***",
    "reading_order" : 17,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000018",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Note that ammonia is basic and the inorganic product is acidic, so there will be a reaction between the two molecules",
    "reading_order" : 18,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000019",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**NH**<sub>**3**</sub>** + HCl → NH**<sub>**4**</sub>**Cl**",
    "reading_order" : 19,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000020",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "We can therefore write the overall equation for the reaction of propanoyl chloride and ammonia as",
    "reading_order" : 20,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000021",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**CH**<sub>**3**</sub>**CH**<sub>**2**</sub>**COCl + 2NH**<sub>**3**</sub>** → CH**<sub>**3**</sub>**CH**<sub>**2**</sub>**CONH**<sub>**2**</sub>** + NH**<sub>**4**</sub>**Cl**",
    "reading_order" : 21,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  } ],
